# `==` on two values of a BARE type parameter is refused in the body (panel 155
# R2, provisional 2026-09-16, ratification queued as `panel 155`).
#
# WHAT IT CLOSES. `same<A>(x: A, y: A)` whose body is `return x == y`, handed a
# `partial` group record, was `check` 0, `build` 0 and **run 134** before this —
# `panic: a partial record has no structural equality` — while `a == b` written
# down is `check` 1. One rule, two answers, depending on whether a generic stood
# in the middle. `spec § 13` promises the refusal "for it and for any value
# holding it", unconditionally, so the compiler was the thing with the bug
# (CLAUDE.md § 12) and the repair costs the specification nothing.
#
# WHY THE BODY AND NOT THE CALL, which is what panel 082 R3 had ruled in August.
# The call-site pass was measured at panel 155 at 284 code lines against an
# estimate of 90-110, needing a second span-keyed table against the standing rule
# at `selfhost/check/state.hero:78-85`, and needing its own `depth > 16` give-up
# with no runtime guard behind it — the defect 035 shape reproduced inside the
# repair. Against it stood four language communities' recorded experience of that
# exact mechanism, Stroustrup's own included: *"The requirements of `sort` on its
# argument type are implicit (hidden) in its function body."*
#
# What was adopted instead was already written in `docs/design/design.md:1721`,
# and no seat had cited it: **"No constraints. No `where`, no bounds. If an
# operation on `T` is needed, pass it as a parameter."** `chooses` below is that
# sentence as a program.
#
# THE RULE IS NARROW AND THE CONTROLS BELOW ARE WHAT SAY SO. It asks whether the
# operand's type IS a type parameter, never whether it REACHES one. Measured
# 2026-09-16: of the 50 generic functions in this tree, two use `==` in a body and
# neither compares two bare type parameters, so this deletes nothing. Widening it
# to `reaches` would delete `tests/golden/run/fixedbugs-a-map-key-that-is-not-itself.hero`
# and `tests/golden/run/abort-map-key-nan.hero`, and that half of the question —
# a type parameter used as a map KEY — is open, panel 155 R3.
# Date: 2026-09-16.
# Bulk regression case.

record Point
    x: i64
    y: i64

# The refusal, at the one line that provokes it.
function same<A>(x: A, y: A) -> bool
    return x == y  #~ generic_equality

# `!=` is the same rule and the same arm, so a case reaching only `==` would be
# half a case.
function differs<A>(x: A, y: A) -> bool
    return x != y  #~ generic_equality

# THE REPAIR, compiled here rather than described: the comparison arrives as a
# parameter, and two parameters of one type are named in the type.
function chooses<A>(x: A, y: A, alike: (function(left: A, right: A) -> bool)) -> bool
    return alike(left: x, right: y)

function points_alike(left: Point, right: Point) -> bool
    return left.x == right.x && left.y == right.y

# THE CONTROLS. If any of these grows a diagnostic the rule has spread past what
# panel 155 adopted, and two goldens in `tests/golden/run/` die with them.

# A map whose KEY is a type parameter: it HOLDS one without being one.
function maps_alike<K>(a: {K: i64}, b: {K: i64}) -> bool
    return a == b

# An array of a type parameter, likewise.
function arrays_alike<A>(xs: [A], ys: [A]) -> bool
    return xs == ys

# A fallible holding one, which is a third container shape and a separate arm.
function maybe_alike<A>(x: A?, y: A?) -> bool
    return x == y

# And a generic that compares something concrete inside itself is untouched: the
# rule is about the operand's type, not about the function being generic.
function counted<A>(xs: [A]) -> bool
    return xs.len() == 0

function main()
    p = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    q = Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    print(same(x: p, y: q))
    print(differs(x: p, y: q))
    print(chooses(x: p, y: q, alike: points_alike))
    m: {str: i64} @ {"a": 1}
    n: {str: i64} @ {"a": 1}
    print(maps_alike(a: m, b: n))
    print(arrays_alike(xs: [1, 2], ys: [1, 2]))
    one: i64? = ok(1)
    two: i64? = ok(1)
    print(maybe_alike(x: one, y: two))
    print(counted([3, 4]))
